<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Federico García Lorca</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a>–<a href="/years/1936">1936</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as García Lorca, Lorca</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Federico García <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorca">Lorca</a> was a Spanish poet, playwright and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> director, born at Fuente Vaqueros near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, and a member of the Generation of '27. He rose to fame with the poems of Romancero gitano (Gypsy Ballads, 1928), which drew on Andalusian life, and after a stay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City from 1929 to 1930 wrote his best-known plays, Blood Wedding (1932), Yerma (1934) and The House of Bernarda Alba (1936). At the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> he was arrested in Granada and killed by Nationalist forces in August 1936. His remains have never been found, and the motive is still disputed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/federico-garcia-lorca.jpg" alt="Federico García Lorca" width="330" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Federico_García_Lorca._Huerta_de_San_Vicente,_Granada.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In poetry T. S. Eliot, Ezra Pound, Wallace Stevens, Marianne Moore and William Carlos Williams wrote in English, and <b>Federico García Lorca</b>, Anna Akhmatova, Constantine Cavafy and Paul Valéry in other languages.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · People</a></li></ul></section>
